      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rub the beef with seasoning. Place in a bowl with the vegetables, bay leaves, allspice, peppercorns, and thyme. Pour over the wine. Cover and refrigerate for at least 4 hours, ideally overnight."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the beef from the marinade. Pat dry. Strain the marinade, keeping the liquid and the vegetables separately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the lard in a dutch oven over high heat. Brown the beef on all sides for 8 to 10 minutes total. Remove. Add the drained vegetables and cook over medium heat for 8 minutes until lightly coloured."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the beef to the pot. Pour in the strained marinade liquid and the beef stock. Add the bay leaves and allspice. Bring to a simmer, cover, and place in the oven at 160C. Braise for 2.5 to 3 hours until the beef is very tender."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the beef to rest for 20 minutes. Discard bay leaves and peppercorns. Transfer the vegetables and all the braising liquid to a blender and puree until completely smooth. Return to the pot, stir in the double cream, mustard, and lemon juice. Simmer for 5 minutes. Slice the beef and serve with the sauce poured over."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Sauce is too thin after blending"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Simmer the sauce over medium heat for 10 to 15 minutes until it reduces and thickens. The cream will help it coat the spoon as it reduces."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Beef is not tender after 3 hours"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return to the oven for another 30 minutes and check again. The marinating and long braise are both essential for sirloin to become tender."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Sauce tastes too sour"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add a small pinch of sugar and a little more cream. The cranberry sauce served alongside balances the flavours at the table."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Svickova is served at weddings, celebrations, and Sunday lunches across the Czech Republic. It is the dish most Czechs name first when asked about their national food. The combination of the creamy vegetable sauce with bread dumplings and cranberry is uniquely Czech. It takes time and patience but the result is worth every step."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
